---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="About" page="about" description="How CATE Analytics filters, clusters, and ranks on-chain token holders.">
  <section class="section">
    <h1>About &amp; Methodology</h1>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        CATE Analytics is a weekly on-chain report for the CATE token. Raw holder lists are freely
        available on GMGN and DexScreener — the value here is that liquidity pools, burn addresses,
        exchange wallets, bridges, and wallet farms are removed before ranking, so you see the
        actual distribution of real holders.
      </p>

      <h2>What gets filtered out</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Liquidity pools (lp)</strong> — Raydium, Pump.fun AMM, and other on-chain
          pool accounts. These hold tokens on behalf of liquidity providers, not as individual
          holders.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Burn addresses (burn)</strong> — Wallets with no private key used to
          permanently remove tokens from circulation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exchanges (exchange)</strong> — Centralised exchange hot wallets and
          custodial aggregation addresses. The exchange holds tokens on behalf of many users;
          it doesn't represent a single holder.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bridges (bridge)</strong> — Cross-chain bridge escrow accounts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bots (bot)</strong> — Volume bot and sandwich bot wallets identified by
          on-chain behaviour patterns.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Filtered wallets are tracked in a <code>wallet_labels</code> table. Labels record their
        source (DexScreener, manual review, or cluster detection) and are never deleted — if a
        classification is wrong, it can be corrected but the history is preserved.
      </p>

      <h2>Cluster collapsing</h2>
      <p>
        Some whales operate many wallets funded from a single source address. The pipeline traces
        the oldest inbound SOL transfer for each top-500 holder. Wallets that share a common
        funder are grouped into a cluster. In the ranked list, each cluster is shown as a single
        entity (the representative wallet with the highest balance), with a <strong>cluster</strong>
        badge and a note showing how many wallets are in the group.
      </p>
      <p>
        Clusters of 3+ wallets are flagged. Infrastructure funders (exchanges, bridges) are
        excluded from cluster grouping so that e.g. wallets all funded via Binance don't get
        incorrectly merged.
      </p>

      <h2>Custodial wallets</h2>
      <p>
        Some on-chain applications pool user tokens in a single wallet (e.g. staking vaults,
        launchpad escrows). These are labelled <strong>custodial</strong>. Unlike exchange wallets
        they are <em>shown</em> in the ranked list — they represent real economic exposure to the
        token — but they are excluded from the accumulator/distributor rankings because their
        balance changes reflect user deposits and withdrawals, not a single entity's conviction.
      </p>

      <h2>Week-over-week diff</h2>
      <p>
        The pipeline takes a snapshot every Sunday. On the second run onward, each holder's
        current balance is compared against the prior snapshot. The "Δ Balance" column shows
        how much a wallet accumulated or distributed. Wallets that buy and fully exit within a
        single week are invisible to the diff by design — they don't appear in either snapshot.
      </p>

      <h2>Data sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Holder balances</strong> — Helius DAS <code>getTokenAccounts</code></li>
        <li><strong>Funder detection</strong> — Helius enhanced transactions endpoint</li>
        <li><strong>Market data</strong> — DexScreener public API (no API key)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Update frequency</h2>
      <p>
        The pipeline runs every Sunday at 18:00 UTC via a GitHub Actions scheduled workflow.
        The site is rebuilt automatically when <code>pipeline/out/weekly.json</code> is
        committed to the main branch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
